::ENG2_U06_L06_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] A slide says, "68 of 120 students remain on campus after dismissal." Which speaker note best explains the evidence? {
~A. This number is here and it is kind of important. #Incorrect. This note is vague. It does not explain the meaning or importance of the evidence.
=B. This result matters because more than half of the surveyed students stay after school, so the campus has a real need for a supervised study space. #Correct. The note explains what the number means and connects it to the presentation purpose.
~C. The slide already says the number, so I do not need to explain it. #Incorrect. Oral communication should interpret important evidence, not simply point to it.
~D. I found a number and put it on the slide. #Incorrect. This note focuses on the presenter's action instead of explaining the evidence to the audience.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] In an integrated oral and digital presentation, what should the slide and speaker note do together? {
~A. The slide should include every word the speaker plans to say. #Incorrect. A slide overloaded with text is hard to follow and weakens oral delivery.
~B. The slide should be decorative, and the speaker should provide all information. #Incorrect. A digital slide should clarify information, not serve only as decoration.
~C. The slide and speaker note should repeat the exact same sentence. #Incorrect. Repetition does not add explanation. The speaker should deepen the audience's understanding.
=D. The slide should show the key evidence, and the speaker note should explain the reasoning. #Correct. This creates a clear partnership between visual support and oral explanation.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is correct for a formal speaker note? {
=A. Students need a supervised study space after school because many remain on campus after dismissal. #Correct. This sentence is complete, formal, and grammatically correct.
~B. Students needs a supervised study space after school. #Incorrect. The subject "students" is plural, so the verb should be "need."
~C. Students need a supervised study space after school because. #Incorrect. This is a fragment because "because" introduces an unfinished reason.
~D. Students need, a supervised study space, after school. #Incorrect. The commas interrupt the sentence incorrectly and make the note less clear.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which speaker note has the most appropriate formal tone? {
~A. So yeah, this slide proves our idea is awesome. #Incorrect. This tone is too casual and exaggerated for an academic presentation.
~B. People should just do what we say because the slide is obvious. #Incorrect. This tone is dismissive and does not explain reasoning.
=C. This evidence supports the proposal because it shows a clear student need. #Correct. This tone is formal, precise, and connected to reasoning.
~D. This is a really cool thing and everyone should like it. #Incorrect. This relies on casual opinion instead of formal explanation.
}

::ENG2_U06_L06_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Before submitting a final oral and digital presentation, what should the student check? {
~A. Whether the slides have the brightest colors possible. #Incorrect. Color is less important than clarity, alignment, and accuracy.
=B. Whether slides, speaker notes, tone, and conventions work together clearly. #Correct. A publication-ready presentation requires the digital and oral parts to support the same message.
~C. Whether the speaker notes are longer than every slide. #Incorrect. Length alone does not show quality. Notes should be purposeful and clear.
~D. Whether every slide uses a different design style. #Incorrect. Inconsistent design can distract from the message.
}
